That PS took effect 1 January 2022, alongside HMT’s revocation of the leverage parts of the CRR.

What’s not yet finalized — and where a residual link to the current Basel 3.1 implementation exists — is confirmed in PS9/24, which states that while the PRA implemented most leverage exposure measure changes in 2022, some changes were not implemented at that time because they relate to changes in the credit risk standardised approach set out in the risk-weighted capital framework, and while the PRA is not including new leverage ratio policy in that near-final PS, the changes to the treatment of off-balance sheet items (i.e. conversion factors) in the credit risk SA chapter will flow through to the leverage framework ￼.

So, comparing old vs. new:

	Pre-2022	Post-Jan 2022 (current)	Basel 3.1 (2027)
Leverage exposure measure core mechanics	Old CRR-based	Updated to align with Basel 3.1 international standards (scope of consolidation, derivatives/SFT treatment, written credit derivatives, etc.)	No new leverage-specific policy — but conversion factors on off-balance sheet items and SA-CCR changes feeding the exposure measure will change automatically, because the exposure measure references the same standardised-approach inputs being revised in PS1/26/PS9/24

Bottom line for your Pillar 1 purposes: there’s no separate PRA consultation or policy statement rewriting the leverage exposure measure denominator alongside PS1/26. Instead, the leverage exposure measure (what practitioners informally call the LBS) will shift indirectly — because it inherits the new off-balance sheet conversion factors and SA-CCR treatment from the credit risk standardised approach chapters being finalized in this Basel 3.1 package, even though the PRA hasn’t issued new leverage-specific rules text.

